    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a> surveyor, militia officer and planter, Washington was made commander-in-chief of the Continental Army in 1775 and held a poorly equipped force together until the British surrender at Yorktown in 1781.</li><li>He gave up his command in 1783, presided over the Constitutional Convention in 1787 and, as the first president from 1789 to 1797, set lasting precedents for the office, among them retirement after two terms.</li><li>He owned slaves all his adult life and freed those he owned outright only by his will, and his campaigns and policies towards Native Americans have drawn sharply different judgements from historians.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Washington left mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> only once, in 1751, when he went with his half-brother Lawrence to Barbados. He caught <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> there, which left his face slightly scarred and made him immune to the disease.</p></aside>
    </section>

